{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,16]],"date-time":"2026-07-16T12:06:40Z","timestamp":1784203600856,"version":"3.55.0"},"reference-count":106,"publisher":"Geological Society of London","issue":"5","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,15]],"date-time":"2026-07-15T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1784073600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["www.lyellcollection.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["JGS"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,9]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    River incision rate data, together with an isostatic unloading model, have been used to constrain the origin of the \u2018staircase\u2019 of Pleistocene river terraces in the Lower Severn Valley. Previous studies show that terrace cyclicity is mainly controlled by climate change, while their \u2018staircase\u2019 pattern is the consequence of crustal uplift: variously accounted for by isostatic unloading, ductile flow in the lower crust and tectonics. Here, it is shown that crustal uplift can be explained by an isostatic unloading model that takes into account the generalized glaciation and deglaciation history of the British Isles, the topography of the Forest of Dean and Cotswold Hills, and the possibility of a lithosphere, rather than asthenosphere, stress relaxation. The model predicts that the flexural response to excavation (i.e. removal) of sediment by meltwater-charged rivers, or ice retreat, following a deglaciation event would be fast initially and then slow. In the Anglian (\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">c<\/jats:italic>\n                    . 450\u2005ka), believed to be the most significant such event, the response is rapid enough to explain the oldest and highest of the Lower Severn Valley terraces (\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">c<\/jats:italic>\n                    . 60\u2005m) and gentle enough to explain, with a small \u2018boost\u2019 from younger events, the height of the youngest and lowest of the terraces (\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">c<\/jats:italic>\n                    . 20\u2005m).\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1144\/jgs2026-026","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,12]],"date-time":"2026-06-12T08:49:36Z","timestamp":1781254176000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1144\/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Origin of river terraces by isostatic unloading of the lithosphere"],"prefix":"10.1144","volume":"183","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2198-2942","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"A. B.","family":"Watts","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Earth Sciences, University of Oxford, South Parks Road, Oxford OX1 3AN, UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"1881","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,15]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_3_7_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1029\/2021GL094040"},{"key":"e_1_3_7_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1126\/sciadv.abn5404"},{"key":"e_1_3_7_4_1","volume-title":"Geology of the Country Around Worcester: Memoir 1:50000 Geological Sheet 199 (England and Wales)","author":"Barclay W.J.","year":"1997","unstructured":"Barclay, W.J., Ambrose, K., Chadwick, R.A. and Pharaoh, T.C. 1997. Geology of the Country Around Worcester: Memoir 1:50\u2009000 Geological Sheet 199 (England and Wales). Stationary Office, London."},{"key":"e_1_3_7_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/j.1365-246X.1984.tb02880.x"},{"key":"e_1_3_7_6_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2307\/1794269"},{"key":"e_1_3_7_7_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.5194\/egusphere-egu25-1210"},{"key":"e_1_3_7_8_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/j.1365-246X.2010.04708.x"},{"key":"e_1_3_7_